 8.52}, {"at_ms": 0.5, "name": "papers_named", "papers": ["moshi"]}, {"at_ms": 2693.9, "name": "verified", "round": 0, "refused": false, "kept": 4, "dropped": []}], "status": "ok", "outcome": "answered", "first_token_ms": 2693.9824000000954, "total_ms": 2693.997100002889, "retries": 0, "regenerations": 0, "tokens_in": 899, "tokens_out": 188, "degraded": false, "queue_wait_ms": 0.07080000068526715, "queue_len_at_arrival": 0, "answer": "Inner Monologue decomposes speech into a chain of text, semantic and acoustic tokens, and predicts this structured sequence in a hierarchical manner [1]. Not only this \u201cInner Monologue\u201d method significantly improves the linguistic quality of generated speech, but we also illustrate how it can provide streaming speech recognition and text-to-speech [2]. As a result, the text stream can provide interesting ways of controlling Moshi, for instance, forcing the sampling of a EPAD token will make Moshi start talking immediately [3]. We furthermore extend this architecture to model several audio streams in parallel, allowing for a conceptually and practically simple handling of full-duplex dialogues with arbitrary dynamics [4].", "citations": ["[1] Moshi (arXiv 2410.00037v1), \u00a72 Related Work, p.5", "[2] Moshi (arXiv 2410.00037v1), \u00a7Abstract, p.1", "[3] Moshi (arXiv 2410.00037v1), \u00a73.4.4 Inner Monologue, p.17", "[4] Moshi (arXiv 2410.00037v1), \u00a71 Introduction, p.3"], "answer_grade": ""}
