 120.0}, {"at_ms": 0.1, "name": "papers_named", "papers": ["moshi"]}, {"at_ms": 3054.5, "name": "verified", "round": 0, "refused": false, "kept": 4, "dropped": []}], "status": "ok", "outcome": "answered", "first_token_ms": 3054.552000001422, "total_ms": 3054.564300000493, "retries": 0, "regenerations": 0, "tokens_in": 1367, "tokens_out": 107, "degraded": false, "queue_wait_ms": 0.03970000398112461, "queue_len_at_arrival": 0, "answer": "For a fair comparison with SemantiCodec and SpeechTokenizer, we also include a downsampled version of our codec in the MUSHRA study [1]. We use Q = 8 quantizers, each with a codebook size of NA = 2048 [2]. Both Transformers use causal masking, which preserves the compatibility of the whole architecture with streaming inference [3]. We report in bold the best performing model trained on less than 2.5T tokens [4].", "citations": ["[1] Moshi (arXiv 2410.00037v1), \u00a75.1 Text Language Modeling, p.23", "[2] Moshi (arXiv 2410.00037v1), \u00a73.3.1 Architecture, p.11", "[3] Moshi (arXiv 2410.00037v1), \u00a73.3.1 Architecture, pp.10-11", "[4] Moshi (arXiv 2410.00037v1), \u00a75.1 Text Language Modeling, p.22"], "answer_grade": ""}
