": 120.0}, {"at_ms": 0.2, "name": "papers_named", "papers": ["hnet"]}, {"at_ms": 2613.1, "name": "verified", "round": 0, "refused": false, "kept": 4, "dropped": []}], "status": "ok", "outcome": "answered", "first_token_ms": 2613.137200001802, "total_ms": 2613.1531999999424, "retries": 0, "regenerations": 0, "tokens_in": 1514, "tokens_out": 197, "degraded": false, "queue_wait_ms": 0.04980000085197389, "queue_len_at_arrival": 0, "answer": "The dechunking layer (Dechunk in equation (3)) consists of a smoothing module and upsampler, as illustrated in Figure 1-( top-right ) [1]. H-Net\u2019s core is a novel dynamic chunking (DC) mechanism which interfaces between the main network and the encoder/decoder networks, learning how to segment data while using standard differentiable optimization [2]. Altogether, H-Net learns segmentation strategies optimized jointly with the main backbone, dynamically compressing input vectors based on contextual information into meaningful chunks [3]. Empirically, the dynamic chunking module naturally compresses data to a similar resolution as BPE tokenizers (4.5-5 bytes/chunk) and qualitatively learns meaningful boundaries, all without any external supervision or heuristics [4].", "citations": ["[1] H-Net (arXiv 2507.07955v2), \u00a72.2.2 Dechunking, pp.6-7", "[2] H-Net (arXiv 2507.07955v2), \u00a71 Introduction, p.2", "[3] H-Net (arXiv 2507.07955v2), \u00a71 Introduction, pp.2-3", "[4] H-Net (arXiv 2507.07955v2), \u00a71 Introduction, pp.3-4"], "answer_grade": ""}
