e_s": 120.0}, {"at_ms": 0.2, "name": "papers_named", "papers": ["hnet"]}, {"at_ms": 2957.4, "name": "verified", "round": 0, "refused": false, "kept": 3, "dropped": []}], "status": "ok", "outcome": "answered", "first_token_ms": 2957.4640999999247, "total_ms": 2957.480299999588, "retries": 0, "regenerations": 0, "tokens_in": 1332, "tokens_out": 192, "degraded": false, "queue_wait_ms": 0.0453000029665418, "queue_len_at_arrival": 0, "answer": "Combined together with the autoregressive prediction loss (i.e., L = LAR + \ud835\udefc \u00cd\ud835\udc46 \u22121 \ud835\udc60=0 L\ud835\udc60 ratio), this mechanism preserves content-adaptive compression: the model learns which vectors to retain based on semantic importance rather than following predetermined patterns, distinguishing H-Net from fixed compression schemes [1]. Without explicit regularization, the model may converge to trivial solutions: either retaining nearly all vectors (negating computational benefits) or compressing excessively (losing critical information) [2]. H-Net\u2019s core is a novel dynamic chunking (DC) mechanism which interfaces between the main network and the encoder/decoder networks, learning how to segment data while using standard differentiable optimization [3].", "citations": ["[1] H-Net (arXiv 2507.07955v2), \u00a72.2.3 Ratio Loss, p.8", "[2] H-Net (arXiv 2507.07955v2), \u00a72.2.3 Ratio Loss, p.8", "[3] H-Net (arXiv 2507.07955v2), \u00a71 Introduction, p.2"], "answer_grade": ""}
